Sure! Moving to Experiment 2 – Containerization of a Python Flask Application Using Docker.

Aim
To develop a Python Flask web application, containerize it using Docker, and run it inside a Docker container.

Procedure
Create a project folder named flask-docker.

Navigate into the project folder.

Create app.py using Notepad.

Write a Flask application that displays Hello.

Create requirements.txt and specify Flask as the dependency.

Create a Dockerfile with the Python base image, working directory, application files, and execution command.

Build the Docker image using docker build.

Verify the image using docker images.

Run the Docker container using port mapping.

Open http://localhost:5000 in a browser.

Verify that the application displays Hello.

Log in to Docker Hub.

Tag the Docker image with the Docker Hub username.

Push the image to Docker Hub.

Verify that the image is available in the Docker Hub repository.

Complete commands
Open CMD and execute these commands one at a time:

mkdir flask-docker
cd flask-docker

notepad app.py
Paste into app.py:

from flask import Flask

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return "Hello"

app.run(host='0.0.0.0')

Save and close Notepad.

Create requirements file:

notepad requirements.txt
Paste:

flask
Save and close.

Create Dockerfile:

notepad Dockerfile
Paste:

FROM python:3.11

WORKDIR /app

COPY . .

RUN pip install flask

EXPOSE 5000

CMD ["python", "app.py"]

Save the file as Dockerfile, ensuring it is not saved as Dockerfile.txt.

Build and run:

docker build -t flask-app .
docker images
docker run -p 5000:5000 flask-app
Open http://localhost:5000 in your browser. Expected output: Hello.

Stop the running container with Ctrl + C in CMD.

Log in and push to Docker Hub:

docker login
docker tag flask-app azcruze/flask-app:v1
docker push azcruze/flask-app:v1
Result
The Flask application was successfully containerized using Docker, executed in a container, and uploaded to Docker Hub.
